{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>You shall not bring the fee of a harlot or the price of a dog, etc.</b> Since dogs too are brazen and consort in public, therefore they know no satiety — for a tiny organ makes the hungry one feel full — and the harlot's brow is brazen, and so the two of them are equal in harlotry and in brazenness. Therefore it is not fitting to bring them to the place of holiness, for there is the boundary of forbidden relations, and there is the head-plate that atones for ״וּמֵצַח אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה הָיָה לָךְ״ (Jeremiah 3:3), while they still persist in their brazenness and in their licentiousness — ״כִּי תוֹעֲבַת ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ גַּם שְׁנֵיהֶם״, that is, both of them alike."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The final phrase 'הַיְנוּ שִׁנּוּיֵיהֶם' is translated literally as 'that is, both of them alike,' reading שִׁנּוּיֵיהֶם as a contracted form of שְׁנֵיהֶם; the reading is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:31.537550826Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}